# Every int crosses the wire signed

**DONE.** The wire has two integers now. `uint` is a `uint64` and
`int` is a `sint64`, and the C++ spelling the declaration already
carries is what says which. Above the boundary both are still a
Python `int`.

## What was measured

`GCOptions.max_freed` is a `U64` whose upstream default is
`std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max()`. Sending it:

    ValueError: Value out of range: 18446744073709551615
    huggorm/wire.py:524

That is the DEFAULT options object - what a caller sends when they
want no limit - so the whole class could not cross an RPC
(`tasks/074`).

## Why it had not fired before

Nothing had ever put a value above 2**63 on the wire. `nar_size` and
`download_size` are u64s and a real NAR size is nowhere near the
limit; `registration_time` is an i64 Unix time. The width has been
academic since the wire existed.

`max_freed` is the first, and only because upstream uses the largest
u64 as a SENTINEL rather than as a size.

## What 074 did instead, and why it is not this

The accessor answers None for the sentinel, so the absence crosses
rather than the number. That is right on its own terms - "no limit" is
not a size, and `PathInfo.registration_time` already reads upstream's
0-means-unknown the same way - and it happens to sidestep the width.

It fixes one field. The next u64 that legitimately exceeds 2**63 has
no sentinel to hide behind.

## The decision: TWO

`sint64` and `uint64`. The alternative - one type plus a build-time
refusal - was written up here as the cheap half worth doing either
way, and it turned out to be unenforceable. A range is a RUN-TIME
fact. "Refuse a `U64` that can exceed 2**63" is either every U64 in
the corpus, which is all six honest ones, or only a literal default,
which catches nothing now that 074 has absorbed the one sentinel.

Goal 1 settles the rest. Nix's fields are `uint64_t`, so a value Nix
can hold has to be a value this binding can carry. Refusing half
their range is not a safe answer that costs a little; it is the
binding disagreeing with the C++ it binds about what the field is.

## What was wrong in this file before

**"A schema change for every existing int field."** No. Only the
unsigned ones move; a `sint64` is still right for every `I64`. Six
fields changed proto type and three did not:

    times_built    nar_size (PathInfo)    nar_size (MissingPaths)
    bytes_freed    download_size          max_freed

    start_time     stop_time              registration_time

That is the whole diff of the emitted C++, and it is four files of
`_wire_fields` strings. Nothing else changed shape.

## Where the width lives now

One place, and it was already there: the `Cxx` spelling on the alias.
`U64` is `Annotated[int, Cxx("uint64_t")]`, and `read.type_of`
attaches that to the `Type` it builds. `Type.wire` used to answer
`self.python`, which is `int` for both aliases; it consults `WIDTHS`
first now.

`Class.parts` is the one reading both the manifest and the emitted
`_wire_fields` ask, so neither can say a width the other does not.

The name `uint` is stated twice - in `read.WIDTHS` and in
`wiretypes.SCALAR_NAMES` - because `huggorm-dsl` may not import the
generator's payload: that file is COPIED into the generated package
and must import nothing of ours. The two disagreeing is not silent.
`model.py` checks every emitted field against `scalar_spelling` and
refuses one it does not know, which is what a renamed `uint` would
be.

## What a parameter cannot say, and why it refuses

A FIELD says its own width. A parameter does not: `params[].type` is
one string, read by the stub emitter as a Python annotation AND by
the schema builder as a wire type. `int` is right for the first and
half-right for the second, and there is no second string to put the
width in.

So a proxy method that takes or returns a `uint64_t` is refused at
build time, by name, pointing here. Nothing declares one - every
integer a service carries today is an `I64` - so the refusal costs
nothing and the wrong answer would have been a silently truncated
number.

Carrying it instead means a second key beside `type`, and `model.py`
cannot reflect one off a compiled class - so `check.py` would diff
the manifest against a shape reflection has no way to produce. That
is `tasks/080`.

A CONTAINER of a width refuses for a different reason. `type_of`
attaches an alias's C++ spelling to the whole annotation, so the
`uint64_t` on `dict[str, U64]` describes what the dict holds. Calling
the dict a `uint` would be wrong and calling it `dict[str, int]`
would lose the top half of every value in it. No declaration writes
one; the branch is written against a test declaration, which is the
only way it is read at all.

## The gates, and which one failed first

- `test_a_limit_above_the_signed_range_crosses_as_itself` sends
  `2**63 + 1` through a real `GCOptionsMsg`. Before the change it
  raised `ValueError: Value out of range: 9223372036854775809`. Not
  the largest u64, on purpose: that one IS the sentinel, so an
  absence would cross and prove nothing.
- `test_each_64_bit_width_reaches_its_own_proto_type` reads the
  descriptor for every value message and checks each declared width
  against it. Derived from the manifest, and it asserts both counts
  are non-zero - a manifest that stopped spelling `uint` would
  otherwise pass by having nothing to check.
- `test_a_field_says_which_64_bit_integer_it_is` asserts the other
  half too: the manifest's PYTHON spelling is `int` for both, so a
  stub never names a type Python does not have.
- The two refusals each have a test declaration, because the corpus
  has neither shape.

All four were seen to FAIL. With `read.WIDTHS` emptied, so the width
reaches nothing:

    4 failed, 239 passed
    test_a_limit_above_the_signed_range_crosses_as_itself
      ValueError: Value out of range: 9223372036854775809
    test_each_64_bit_width_reaches_its_own_proto_type
      no field crosses unsigned; the width is unproven
    test_a_field_says_which_64_bit_integer_it_is
      ('total', 'int') != ('total', 'uint')
    test_a_container_of_a_width_is_refused_rather_than_guessed
      DID NOT RAISE TypeError

The two refusals were perturbed on their own as well, and each gave
the wrong answer it exists to stop: `UNCROSSABLE = ()` let
`fits(limit)` cross as `int`, and dropping the container guard named
`dict[str, U64]` a `uint` - a dict declared to be a scalar.

Whole suite: 251 passed, up from 246.

Opened 2026-09-02, while closing `tasks/074`. Closed the same day.
